= Nonviolent Communication — Technical =

'''Nonviolent Communication (NVC)''' is Rosenberg's humanistic, needs-oriented approach to interpersonal communication. It is a pedagogical and normative framework, not a mechanistic psychological theory with a uniquely established causal pathway.<ref name="RosenbergPrimary">Rosenberg, Marshall B. ''Nonviolent Communication: A Language of Life'', introductory chapter, PuddleDancer Press (2003). https://www.naturalchild.org/articles/marshall_rosenberg/giving_from_the_heart.pdf</ref>

== Historical provenance and conceptual architecture ==
Marshall B. Rosenberg developed NVC in the context of humanistic psychology and conflict-oriented communication training. Its familiar four components are '''observation, feeling, need, request''' (OFNR). The Center for Nonviolent Communication also describes a broader practice involving self-connection, empathic presence, honest expression, and noncoercive influence; the four components should not be mistaken for the entire model.<ref name="CNVCPrimary">Center for Nonviolent Communication. ''The Purpose of NVC''. https://www.cnvc.org/about/purpose-of-nvc</ref>

=== Operational distinctions ===
{| class="wikitable"
! Component !! Training operation !! Common category error
|-
| Observation || Describe a time- and context-specific event || Infer motive or character as if directly observed
|-
| Feeling || Identify one's emotion or sensation || Present a belief about another person as a feeling
|-
| Need || Identify an underlying concern or valued condition || Treat a particular strategy as the only possible need
|-
| Request || Propose a concrete, voluntary action || Disguise a threat or penalty as a request
|}

The distinctions are interpretive training conventions. Different people can reasonably disagree about an event's description, an emotion label, or whether a request is freely rejectable. A request's voluntariness depends partly on the surrounding power relationship, not solely on its grammar.

== Worked dialogue analysis ==
'''Invented training vignette, not empirical data.''' A supervisor says, "You are careless with deadlines." This attributes a stable trait without specifying an observation. A more operational formulation is: "The report arrived on Thursday rather than the agreed Tuesday; I am concerned because the team needs review time. Could we agree on a status update by Monday?" The revision identifies a verifiable event, the speaker's concern and an actionable proposal. It does '''not''' prove that the employee will respond favorably, that the deadline was reasonable, or that the supervisor has resolved structural causes.

A facilitator can separately invite the other person's account, constraints, and alternative strategies. A coercive workplace relationship may make apparent consent unreliable; communication technique does not eliminate unequal authority.

== Evaluation and causal inference ==
The estimand in a controlled NVC-training evaluation should specify a population, intervention dose and fidelity, comparator, outcome and follow-up horizon. For example, comparing a facilitated NVC course with another equally intensive communication course addresses a narrower question than comparing trainees with an untreated group. Unblinded self-reports may capture expectations, social desirability or shared-method bias. Cluster assignment, attrition, trainer allegiance and spillover can complicate causal inference.

Lazzari and colleagues' systematic review (2026) identified 31 quantitative studies and more than 2,700 participants. In pre/post designs they reported moderate evidence for increased self-compassion, low evidence for empathy and communication competence, and very low evidence for reductions in anger or aggression. In intervention-versus-control comparisons, they reported moderate evidence for greater self-reported empathy and communication competence, low evidence for self-compassion, and very low evidence for anger or aggression outcomes. These are the review authors' evidence ratings, not universal effect sizes or proof of efficacy in every setting.<ref name="LazzariReview">Lazzari, G.; Jean, B. R.; de la Sablonnière, R.; van Vugt, F. T. (2026). "A systematic review of quantitative studies testing Nonviolent Communication training interventions." ''Frontiers in Psychology'' 17:1800455. doi:10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1800455. https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1800455/full</ref>

A concrete primary study by Wacker and Dziobek compared a workplace training group with a no-training comparison group and examined communication behavior, empathy-related outcomes and social stressors; it was not a randomized comparison with an equally intensive active course.<ref name="Wacker">R. Wacker and I. Dziobek, [https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27977228/ Preventing empathic distress and social stressors at work through nonviolent communication training: A field study with health professionals], ''Journal of Occupational Health Psychology'' 23, 141–150 (2018).</ref> The 2026 synthesis emphasizes the predominance of self-report and inadequate controls, not independently observed general conflict resolution.

A 2024 scoping review mapped NVC in healthcare interpersonal relationships, but mapping reported uses and studies is not equivalent to a pooled causal efficacy estimate.<ref name="HealthcareReview">"Non-violent communication as a technology in interpersonal relationships in health work: a scoping review." ''BMC Health Services Research'' 24:289 (2024). doi:10.1186/s12913-024-10753-2. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10916228/</ref>

== Boundaries and alternatives ==
Active listening, motivational interviewing, mediation and structured feedback may share certain conversational practices with NVC, but they differ in aims, training and evidence base. Comparative effectiveness cannot be inferred from conceptual resemblance. NVC language may be unsuitable when immediate safety action, enforceable boundaries, reporting obligations or specialist treatment is required. Its reported uses include interpersonal training, education, organizational facilitation and healthcare, but deployment is not evidence of effectiveness. Its universal-needs vocabulary is a philosophical and pedagogical commitment, not proof that all people classify needs identically across cultures.

== Research questions ==
Future comparative trials should preregister outcomes, assess implementation fidelity, use independent behavioral measures where feasible, evaluate long-term maintenance and harms, and test transportability across languages, power structures and organizational settings. Self-reported empathy and observed conflict behavior should not be treated as interchangeable endpoints.

